{
  "configuration_id": "config_003",
  "reason": "No prior evidence exists, so the first experiment must establish a strong baseline and reduce uncertainty about the most impactful design axes: (1) whether multimodal (RNA+image) outperforms single-modality, (2) whether the strongest available encoders (scgpt for RNA, ViT-L for image) provide a meaningful advantage, and (3) whether multiprofile aggregation helps. config_003 (multimodal, multiprofile, scgpt, ViT-L, avgpool, weighted) tests all three of these hypotheses simultaneously while using avgpool aggregation instead of the more expensive transformer, keeping training cost in the upper-moderate range (roughly 70-80% of the most expensive configs) without sacrificing the ability to aggregate multiple profiles. If it performs well, subsequent experiments can test whether upgrading to transformer aggregation (config_007) or swapping RNA encoders yields further gains. If it underperforms, the result localizes the problem to modality combination, encoder choice, or aggregation strategy, guiding the remaining 9 experiments efficiently. Weighted fusion is preferred over concat as it allows the model to learn modality-specific importance, which is particularly useful when RNA and image signals may have unequal informativeness."
}

